{
  "dom_id": "step-q132426",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find the sum modulo $33$ of the solutions to the congruence $15x≡18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,33).$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*",
  "worked_solution": "In the given congruence, we have $a=15$ and $n=33$, and so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(15,33)=3.\n$$\n\nSince $b=18$ is divisible by $3,$ our congruence has $3$ solutions.\n\nFirst, we use the division property of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15x & ≡18 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,33) \\\\ 3\\cdot 5x & ≡3\\cdot 6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 11) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot 5x & ≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot 6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 11) \\\\ 5x & ≡6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in the new congruence above, we have ${a}_{1}=5$ and ${n}_{1}=11$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}({a}_{1},{n}_{1})=\\text{gcd}(5,11)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence has a unique solution modulo $11.$\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$ And to find the inverse of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11),$ we compute the exponents of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2} & ≡25≡2\\cdot 11+3≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {5}^{3} & ≡3\\cdot 5≡15≡11+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {5}^{4} & ≡4\\cdot 5≡20≡11+9≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {5}^{5} & ≡9\\cdot 5≡45≡4\\cdot 11+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $9\\cdot 5≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11),$ which means that $9$ is the multiplicative inverse of $5$ (modulo $11$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & ≡6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ 9\\cdot 5x & ≡9\\cdot 6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ (9\\cdot 5)x & ≡54 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ x & ≡54 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ x & ≡4\\cdot 11+10 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ x & ≡10 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the solution $x≡10\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$ \n\nBut our original congruence was modulo $33$ (not modulo $11$). To find all the solutions modulo $33,$ we repeatedly add $11$ modulo $33$ until we reach the number $10$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ≡10 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,33) \\\\ {x}_{2} & ≡10+11≡21 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,33) \\\\ {x}_{3} & ≡21+11≡32 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,33) \\\\ {x}_{4} & ≡32+11≡43≡1\\cdot 33+10≡10≡{x}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,18)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x≡10,21,32\\,(\\text{mod}\\,33).$ \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n10+21+32≡63≡33+30≡30\\,(\\text{mod}\\,33).\n$$",
